#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078279 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/id #uuid "06d5e05a-4595-551f-8c1b-7c585f69b47b" :question/math-academy-id "e-74" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4} }.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-2\\sqrt{x}}{x-4} & =\\frac{4-2\\sqrt{4}}{4-4}=\\frac{0}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4} &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {(x -2 \\sqrt{x})(x+2\\sqrt x)} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n            &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x^2-\\left(2\\sqrt{x}\\right)^2} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x^2-4x} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x(x-4)} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x} {x+2\\sqrt x} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {4} {4+2\\sqrt4} \\\n\t    &=\\dfrac 1 2.\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078280 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "7db311cd-70a0-50f4-8984-4a578abc67eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-75" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac {3x} {\\sqrt{x +1} -1}  }.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{x+1}-1} & =\\frac{3\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{0+1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac {3x} {\\sqrt{x + 1} - 1} \n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(3x)(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{x + 1} - 1)(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{\\left(\\sqrt{x+1}\\right)^2-1^2}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{x+1-1}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{x}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}3(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)\\\n&=3(\\sqrt{0+1}+1)\\\n\t&=6.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b52a57-d327-59a4-87f1-8f2c1f94c3ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297006} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc99c1f-75f5-59ab-9047-139852f97b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17b52a57-d327-59a4-87f1-8f2c1f94c3ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297006} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2f5daf8-e978-513a-b567-a86a92f7824d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92252b75-35d4-5466-b5eb-58457897a6af" :question/math-academy-id "q-338481" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8x}}{x-8}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to }\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8x}}{x-8} & =\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8(8)}}{8-8}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8x}}{x-8} & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{(8-\\sqrt{8x})(8+\\sqrt{8x})}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{8}^{2}-(\\sqrt{8x})^{2}}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{64-8x}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8(x-8)}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8\\cancel{(x-8)}}{\\cancel{(x-8)}(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8}{8+\\sqrt{8x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{8+\\sqrt{8(8)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{8+8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "333dc7a9-ef74-53f8-ae90-5e5e7294c3b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297010} {:answer/id #uuid "adfbf7e5-724e-570d-a920-f0b2140fdbf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adfbf7e5-724e-570d-a920-f0b2140fdbf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297011} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9ce42d7-ba17-543a-ad4c-ba3d8f394931" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6417b838-8f56-5a40-8b5a-45b5e7ecb695" :question/math-academy-id "q-338484" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}{{3}^{2}-3-6}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{({x}^{2}-x-6)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x+1)-4}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(3+2)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83e713a7-3de6-53c4-b9e2-14eed83e7e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83e713a7-3de6-53c4-b9e2-14eed83e7e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297014} :answer-field/id #uuid "b39d826d-10a5-561f-a5c0-b456268562a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cea8ecd3-a2e6-51b4-876e-28c17aeaa239" :question/math-academy-id "q-338496" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{(4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x-\\sqrt{x})(2x+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x)^{2}-(\\sqrt{x})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{4{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x(4x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{\\cancel{(4x-1)}(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x\\cancel{(4x-1)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{2x+\\sqrt{x}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5b4e8a5-a2cc-5f7c-9777-ff4b3cf574bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264522} {:answer/id #uuid "a5cfa902-4e4d-535a-9d48-b74e92e2614f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264523} {:answer/id #uuid "df00b693-51c6-5ee8-8e2d-3ad97cd23022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5b4e8a5-a2cc-5f7c-9777-ff4b3cf574bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264522} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc7bd336-dc82-597d-938b-ca1bda73e817" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f682dc1-21d5-50c6-a843-a82f4d54fb3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338515" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{z-6}{\\sqrt{3z-2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $z=6,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{z-6}{\\sqrt{3z-2}-4} & =\\frac{6-6}{\\sqrt{3(6)-2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{z-6}{\\sqrt{3z-2}-4} & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(\\sqrt{3z-2}-4)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(\\sqrt{3z-2})^{2}-{4}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(3z-2)-16} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{3z-18} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{3(z-6)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{\\sqrt{3z-2}+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(6)-2}+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a33edddc-1da0-5d41-a93b-892dcdc07dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076258} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8cefe5-a5a0-5541-932b-e9e5fbe534ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076259} {:answer/id #uuid "df7ddb01-dc80-5476-aa94-7fc2b5a0142e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076260} {:answer/id #uuid "2d727eff-da34-5178-8fa7-980bad27e56b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076261} {:answer/id #uuid "63eb7fd8-208e-52bd-8b1c-8c3561420d5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd8cefe5-a5a0-5541-932b-e9e5fbe534ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076259} :answer-field/id #uuid "a542e28e-89c7-5fa7-9efa-480f95f1bcb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "999c69b4-b8e1-5935-8b29-a7c23a4a8300" :question/math-academy-id "q-4463" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0+4}-2}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+4}-2)(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x+4-4}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{0+4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8298da89-4e8a-5c30-9d6a-3fa48a89ff90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076264} {:answer/id #uuid "bccaf12b-6c9b-585b-84f9-0268e20d16f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076265} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9ad3e0-dcfd-5a40-89f8-13bb63dad038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076266} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0b29b4-ffb0-543e-9824-22aad58d4ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076267} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea9b4f1-cf27-556d-b1b6-b28ad28fedf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bccaf12b-6c9b-585b-84f9-0268e20d16f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076265} :answer-field/id #uuid "390ea65e-f475-5aa4-85d6-c92c4650e8e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fb5c930-d58c-5aeb-b5e0-b4a28b9ae989" :question/math-academy-id "q-4464" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+3}-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+3}-2} & =\\frac{1-1}{\\sqrt{1+3}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+3}-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+3}-2)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{{(\\sqrt{x+3})}^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{x+3-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(\\sqrt{x+3}+2) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3}+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7148833d-dcfc-556d-98a2-769bb8ee8426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297018} {:answer/id #uuid "3e41d188-2da4-545b-92dc-0f528eac4be7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297019} {:answer/id #uuid "f869a21b-2e40-5816-82a8-baeef2ab7329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297020} {:answer/id #uuid "b88d5cc7-80ea-5684-ac5b-b552366580ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297021} {:answer/id #uuid "23e859d6-7273-5354-bf41-a04a27d7d07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7148833d-dcfc-556d-98a2-769bb8ee8426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297018} :answer-field/id #uuid "718dc51d-f545-58cd-80c2-cb003c08fff4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d110047-c802-560a-bccf-6cce5aa93b1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-4467" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}} & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{0+5}-\\sqrt{5}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{(\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5})(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{{(\\sqrt{x+5})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{x+5-5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+5}+\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97c72a49-54ee-5327-b262-1da182913087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297025} {:answer/id #uuid "d30fde5e-d206-5202-a26f-acd1fe1edf47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297026} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb6096f-ccc3-5e14-85a3-641e0c545f08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297027} {:answer/id #uuid "24508188-12d5-5aa1-9f0e-d360dba65b3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297028} {:answer/id #uuid "83ba7d0d-e082-55c3-b59b-b9607f7f2668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97c72a49-54ee-5327-b262-1da182913087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297025} :answer-field/id #uuid "a85e83e9-1e14-5fff-9cf1-d4709bc85d84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ff5a4e0-9857-5121-92cf-e9b6328f9ca8" :question/math-academy-id "q-4468" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-9}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-9} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}{{3}^{2}-9}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-9} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{(x-3)(x+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+1})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x+1-4}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(3+3)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3b6802a-8241-5473-88a4-1e17cdf7cf03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297032} {:answer/id #uuid "fb042f75-3b70-5bf9-a61f-c4b40effbfbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297033} {:answer/id #uuid "868333c8-3e21-5181-af98-43c91f951bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297034} {:answer/id #uuid "a787e181-1b60-5d74-b492-e3026fe1c9b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297035} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1ea518-9637-5a50-8e25-922e5f877d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb042f75-3b70-5bf9-a61f-c4b40effbfbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297033} :answer-field/id #uuid "a122ba64-39a8-5687-b975-015093c46c45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7758f03-f7d8-5681-80dc-022d1d05b2a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-4471" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{1-1}{1-\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{(x-\\sqrt{x})(x+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{{x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{x(x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x+\\sqrt{x}}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1+\\sqrt{1}}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8177625e-0fcd-5d78-8f77-7ad6c29a7ae2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264533} {:answer/id #uuid "e658e508-987f-5a4f-8f2e-ec2f8203d053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264534} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2e0f3a-8505-59a5-925d-db93ef752c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264535} {:answer/id #uuid "22179b09-8761-585b-871e-5bb6d504d869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264536} {:answer/id #uuid "3e47abe8-ec8c-5c9c-b680-47a9eb83e40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e47abe8-ec8c-5c9c-b680-47a9eb83e40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264537} :answer-field/id #uuid "846cf5ca-7696-5b32-a145-e9f822d54432" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51433638-23de-5865-9a91-de5431948eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-46831" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2+2}-2}{{2}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+2}-2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+2})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x+2-4}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2+2)(\\sqrt{2+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
